well we are certainly paying more than  we'd like to for gas but how good is the  fuel we're actually putting into our  cars well it is a question the 8 news  now i-team wanted to answer we sent  samples from all across our valley to  get tested and along the way we learned  how gas stations are keeping this  commodity safe  with record gas prices  we wanted to know are las vegas gas  stations providing quality fuel  our project started with getting samples  from 12 different locations we took just  enough to fill up even smaller  containers to send to two different labs  each one sealed labeled wrapped and  packaged  then all our samples were off what do  you guys do in terms of the gasoline  testing it's primarily designed to catch  things like water contamination or dirt  contamination ryan stark is the  president of one of the labs we paid to  test your gas blackstone laboratories is  based in indiana they took our samples  running each one for water and other  deficiencies are most of the samples you  guys test  good gas  usually stuff coming out of the tent out  of the pumps pretty good  and that's what we found here's how each  sample turned out we started with the  green valley grocery on charleston in  buffalo  next we tested the funnies at galwin and  rancho  next the 76 at patrick and rainbow  the smiths at windmill in eastern  the rebel at flamingo in paradise  the arco at fremont in eastern  the sinclair near cheyenne and losi  the chevron at charleston and rancho  the terribles at west cliff in rainbow  the 76 at sahara in fort apache  the shell at twain in fort apache  and finally the sinclair at warm springs  in gillespie  we're in a dry environment so to have  water intrusion is very very unlikely  david crawford is president of green  valley grocery one of the largest gas  station companies in the valley and home  to two of our testing locations if you  had water contamination you're going to  have broken down vehicles and  you can't have that crawford tells us  why it's so important for stations like  his to make sure water and other debris  isn't getting into his tanks we found  out it's really hard for that to happen  so every tank in the ground is basically  a tank inside of a tank first crawford  showed us this panel which monitors the  amount of water in his tanks each tank  holding tens of thousands of gallons of  fuel bound for your car anytime that  number is anything besides zero this  tank is going to set off an alarm it  also will email company leaders  notifying them of an issue but there are  even more safeguards we're always here  we're always working outside crawford  shows us these filters in the pumps that  will swell up if they detect water and  the massive tanks themselves are sealed  and double lined and then the space in  between the two tanks has monitoring on  it so if there's any liquid of any kind  whether it be water or fuel it will set  off an alarm the fuel coming into the  valley is also not an issue two  pipelines from california and utah bring  gasoline to the valley it's then mixed  with each company's additives their  special sauce per se before it is sent  on its way to a gas station near you  that's why we go through all these steps  to keep the fuel safe ultimately we  learned most gasoline issues aren't  occurring at the pump  but are happening after the gas is  already in your car issues can arise  when someone puts water in your tank or  somehow sediment gets into your engine  take it from the guy who tests it it  can't happen but most of the time it's  in good shape and the samples that we  see are kind of outliers because they're  looking for problems and sometimes there  are problems in there  and denise brought up a good point she  said why didn't you go to costco and  truthfully it's because the line was so  long it was going to take way too much  time so there are professionals who go  out and basically do what we just did  and get those gas samples that test is  usually just a visual one looking for  water in sediment in like a clear bottle  coming up on thursday night we're going  to show you how those inspectors do  their jobs and how and when they cite  stations that's coming up thursday right  here on 8 news now
 

